WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.282
ASP.

2
00:00:01.282 --> 00:00:06.793
NET Core includes rate-limiting middleware for defining and applying request policies.

3
00:00:06.793 --> 00:00:13.842
At a high level, teams can configure global behavior, named policies, and endpoint-specific policy attachment.

4
00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:23.262
The important design question is not only whether rate limiting exists, but whether the policy matches the endpoint and the users who depend on it.

5
00:00:23.262 --> 00:00:28.710
Global policies can be useful when the entire application needs a basic safety floor.

6
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:34.029
They can also become too blunt if the application has very different request types.

7
00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:48.896
An inexpensive read endpoint, authentication endpoint, expensive search, upload path, report generator, tenant API, and public API may all deserve different limits because they create different load and user experience expectations.

8
00:00:48.896 --> 00:00:51.716
Named policies make intent easier to review.

9
00:00:51.716 --> 00:01:02.482
A policy name should describe the relationship it protects, such as an API client policy, an authenticated user policy, a report-generation policy, or an upload policy.

10
00:01:02.482 --> 00:01:09.467
Reviewers should be able to connect the policy to endpoint cost, expected traffic, and operational ownership.

11
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:17.541
Endpoint-specific policy attachment is often helpful because the rate-limit decision appears near the route or endpoint group.

12
00:01:17.541 --> 00:01:22.284
Minimal APIs, controllers, and route groups can make policy scope visible.

13
00:01:22.284 --> 00:01:32.729
That visibility matters during code review because a future developer should understand why an endpoint has a stricter or looser policy than neighboring endpoints.

14
00:01:32.729 --> 00:01:36.831
Configuration values should be reviewable and environment-aware.

15
00:01:36.831 --> 00:01:40.483
Local development limits may be relaxed for productivity.

16
00:01:40.483 --> 00:01:42.919
Staging may need test-friendly values.

17
00:01:42.919 --> 00:01:49.519
Production should reflect real capacity, user experience, tenant fairness, and infrastructure behavior.

18
00:01:49.519 --> 00:01:54.005
Limits should not be hidden constants that no one owns or understands.

19
00:01:54.005 --> 00:01:58.235
Rate limiting is most useful when it is paired with observability.

20
00:01:58.235 --> 00:02:10.987
Teams should be able to see when a policy is triggered, which endpoint was involved, which partition was affected, and whether the limit is protecting the system or masking a deeper capacity problem.
